// ScaleCorpus — the generated ladder the scale suite measures.
//
// ONE PROGRAM PER RUNG, and each rung DOUBLES all eight structural knobs at once. Six rungs is a
// 32x span in six compiles. The alternative — a matrix, one axis per knob — would be eight ladders
// and forty-eight compiles to learn the same thing, because the question is never "which knob is
// the compiler slow in" (the per-phase table answers that already) but "does the whole thing stay
// linear as a program grows", and a program grows in every direction at once. (Two SHIFT shapes ride
// along that are COVERAGE, not knobs — they are fixed-size and do not double; see `shiftCoverageSource`.)
//
// ⚠ THE CORPUS NOW DECLARES ITSELF. Every run prints `printCorpusManifest` — the constructs it
// generates, the dimensions it scales, and, load-bearing, the ones it structurally CANNOT see (it
// measures memory, never time; it compiles a subset of Maxon, not the whole language). The corpus was
// blind in four measured dimensions at once and every blind spot was silent; an instrument that cannot
// name what it cannot see keeps producing confident zeros.
//
// ARCHITECTURE.md claims compilation is linear in program size, and quotes measurements to back it —
// 1.96 -> ~1.0 for a 3,200-`if` function, 40x faster, a 19,200-block function that used to
// SIGSEGV. Every one of those numbers was measured on throwaway generated programs that were never
// committed. This file is the corpus that makes those claims falsifiable, and the two headline
// shapes are deliberately reproduced at the top rung: rung 5's `longFunction` is 3,200 `if`s, and
// its `deepBlocks` is ~19,200 blocks.
//
// ===========================================================================================
// THE TRAP, AND WHY EVERY VALUE HERE IS SEEDED FROM SOMETHING OPAQUE
// ===========================================================================================
//
// `foldConstOperands` is a real pass in this pipeline. A naively generated benchmark — the obvious
// one, a pile of arithmetic on literals — FOLDS AWAY TO NOTHING. The suite would then measure an
// empty compile at every rung, fit a perfect straight line through it, and report that the
// compiler is beautifully linear. It is the "check the instrument before the subject" failure, and
// it produces the most convincing possible wrong answer.
//
// So every value chain in this corpus starts from something the folder provably cannot see
// through, and there are exactly two such things in shv2:
//
//   1. A LOOP-CARRIED `var`. Its value at the top of the loop is a phi of its initializer and
//      whatever the body last wrote, so it is not a constant — and shv2 has no SCCP to prove
//      otherwise. `scaleSeed` is one, and it is where every rung's data originates.
//   2. A CALL RESULT. shv2 has no inliner and no interprocedural constant propagation, so a
//      function's return value is opaque at the call site no matter how trivial the callee. Every
//      generated function therefore takes its seed as a PARAMETER and derives everything from it.
//
// The runner does not take this on trust. `assertCorpusCompiled` (below) re-verifies it on every
// single ladder, and a corpus that folded flat is a BROKEN RUN — the one thing this instrument
// exits non-zero for, because it means nothing was measured at all.
//
// ===========================================================================================
// A RUNG DIRECTORY HOLDS EXACTLY THE GENERATED SOURCES, AND NOTHING ELSE
// ===========================================================================================
//
// This is not tidiness. It is the reproducibility the whole instrument rests on, and it was BROKEN.
//
// `build <dir>` LISTS that directory, and every entry it finds costs the compiler a `FilePath` and a
// `String` — measured, exactly, at +13 allocations and +459 bytes per extra file, at every rung. The
// runner used to write each rung's EXECUTABLE and its METRICS TSV into the rung's own source
// directory, and `.scale-tmp/` is never cleaned. So the first run on a fresh checkout compiled a
// ten-file directory and every run after it compiled a twelve-file one, and the two disagreed by 26
// allocations and 945 bytes per rung.
//
// The numbers were therefore "bit-for-bit reproducible" only if you never deleted your temp
// directory — which is to say they were not reproducible at all, and the committed goldens that
// gated on them would have failed on any machine that had never run the suite before. (Found by
// exactly the delta report this instrument now prints, on a run that changed no compiler code.)
//
// The outputs now live in the corpus ROOT, outside every rung (see ScaleTestRunner), and
// `pruneRungDir` guarantees the invariant holds even for a `.scale-tmp` left behind by an older
// build. ⚠ It DELETES: `<root>/rung<N>/` is owned by this generator, including under
// `--emit-corpus=<dir>`.
//
// ===========================================================================================
// NOT COMMITTED, ON PURPOSE
// ===========================================================================================
//
// The rungs are generated into `.scale-tmp/` at run time (gitignored, exactly as `.spec-tmp/` is).
// Committing them would add megabytes of generated Maxon to the tree for no benefit: generation is
// deterministic, so the corpus is a pure function of this file, and this file IS the corpus.
// `--emit-corpus=<dir>` dumps a ladder anywhere you want to look at it.
//
// THE SUBSET: shv2 implements `int` arithmetic, functions (<=6 params), `if`, `while`, `break`,
// `return`, `let`, `var`, bitwise + shifts (`and`/`shl`/`shr`), and ranged typealiases. No `print`, no
// structs, no arrays, no strings, no `for`, no `as` casts. Everything below stays inside it — and
// `printCorpusManifest` states exactly this boundary on every run, so a construct outside it (and the
// codegen change to it that would be invisible) is NAMED rather than silently absent.

// Which rung of the ladder, and how many of them. A rung DOUBLES the one below it, so these are
// exponents, not sizes: `--rungs=20` would not be a big run, it would be a program a hundred
// thousand times rung 0 that never finishes. Hence a hard, refused-not-clamped upper bound.
export typealias RungIndex = int(0 to 15)
export typealias RungCount = int(1 to 16)

export let MinRungCount = 1 as RungCount
export let MaxRungCount = 8 as RungCount
export let DefaultRungCount = 6 as RungCount

// Total bytes of generated source in one rung — the denominator of the degeneracy check.
export typealias SourceByteCount = int(0 to u32.max)

// The outcome of parsing `--rungs=<n>`. A union rather than a throw, because the caller is inside
// the argument loop and must neither diverge nor invent a value: `invalid` is recorded as a bad
// option and the driver then refuses to run at all.
export union RungCountArg
	valid(count RungCount)
	invalid
end 'RungCountArg'

// Parse `--rungs=<n>`.
//
// A rung count outside the ladder's range is REFUSED, never clamped. Each rung DOUBLES the program,
// so `--rungs=20` is not "a thorough run", it is a program a hundred thousand times rung 0 that will
// never finish — and a caller who asked for it and silently got 8 would believe they had measured
// something they had not.
//
// The digits themselves are `parseCount`'s job (ScaleTestRunner) — this only decides what a valid
// RANGE is. Two hand-rolled decimal loops in one suite would be one loop too many.
export function parseRungCount(raw String) returns RungCountArg
	let value = try parseCount(raw) otherwise 'notANumber'
		return RungCountArg.invalid
	end 'notANumber'

	if value < MinRungCount or value > MaxRungCount 'outOfRange'
		return RungCountArg.invalid
	end 'outOfRange'
	return RungCountArg.valid(value as RungCount)
end 'parseRungCount'

// The number of `if`s, files, functions... in one knob at one rung. Bounded well above the top
// rung's largest knob (rung 7's deepBlocks is 200 << 7 = 25,600).
export typealias KnobSize = int(1 to 1048576)

// ===========================================================================================
// The eight knobs, and their rung-0 sizes
// ===========================================================================================
//
// Each is `base << rung`. The bases are tuned so that rung 0 clears fixed process + stdlib
// overhead (a rung that compiles in 3 ms measures the OS, not the compiler) while the top rung stays
// tractable. They are the ONE place to retune if the ladder gets too slow or too fast.

// The realistic codebase shape: many small functions. Each is a few ops — a couple of arithmetic
// ops and a branch — which is what most real functions are.
let FunctionsBase = 16 as KnobSize

// Real source FILES, not one big one: this is what exercises `load`, `merge`, and the query spine,
// none of which a single-file corpus would touch at all. The `manyFunctions` above are distributed
// across them, so functions-per-file stays constant as both double.
let FilesBase = 4 as KnobSize

// ONE function, N sequential `if`s. The 3,200-`if` benchmark ARCHITECTURE.md quotes: at rung 5
// this is exactly 100 << 5 = 3,200.
let LongIfsBase = 100 as KnobSize

// ONE function, a huge BLOCK count. Each `if`/`else` contributes three blocks (then, else,
// continuation), so this is ~3x its knob: at rung 5, 200 << 5 = 6,400 if/elses = ~19,200 blocks —
// the shape that used to SIGSEGV, and the reason "no CFG traversal may recurse" is a rule. It is
// separate from `longFunction` because that one grows OPS in a mostly-straight line and this one
// grows the CFG.
let DeepBlocksBase = 200 as KnobSize

// N chained calls, each caller holding values live ACROSS its call — so this is the cross-call
// spill bracket knob, not merely a call-depth knob.
let CallChainBase = 8 as KnobSize

// ONE function, N pressured loops. Its base is the smallest of the six because its cost used to
// overtake everything else as the ladder climbed — TWICE, from two different sources, both now fixed.
//
// THIS KNOB IS LINEAR NOW, and the history is worth keeping because the comment here was WRONG in two
// successive ways, each naming a real bug that was then fixed elsewhere:
//
//   1. It read "KNOWN SUPERLINEAR: the splitter's driver recomputes liveness from scratch after every
//      split… regalloc:liveness grows x3.69 per doubling." True until `49b160ce2` made that driver
//      INCREMENTAL. `regalloc:liveness` reads x1.99 now.
//   2. Then the real bend was the PARSER: `parseWhileStatement` minted one header phi per mutable var
//      IN SCOPE, so a function whose `var`s accumulate paid Σk ≈ L²/2 phis — minted, carried through
//      four passes inflating every dense column sized by the block-arg id space, and then deleted by
//      `pruneDeadBlockArgs`. Fixed by `11b9bbcba`: a loop now carries a phi only for the vars its
//      extent ASSIGNS. `pruneDeadBlockArgs` reads x1.99, `elimTrivialBlockArgs` x1.97, and
//      `phase:parse` x1.99 — the tail is gone at the source, not subtracted from the reading.
//
// THE KNOB IS RIGHT TO STAY AS IT IS. A function with many loops and many mutable vars in scope is
// ordinary code, and this knob held the front end honest until the front end earned it: it is what
// made the O(V×B) SSA-construction cost visible, and it is what will catch the next thing that inflates
// the block-arg id space. What it must NOT carry is a claim about which pass is slow — twice that claim
// outlived the bug it named. If this knob bends again, MEASURE which phase, do not read this comment.
let PressuredLoopsBase = 2 as KnobSize

// ONE function, ONE chain of loops NESTED to depth N — distinct from `pressuredLoops`, which grows
// loop COUNT at depth 1. Depth is what the loop-phi token pre-scan climbs: `parseWhileStatement`
// re-derives each loop's extent from the tokens (`loopBodyEndIndex`) and reads its assigned names off
// them (`namesAssignedIn`), and a loop NESTED inside another has its tokens re-scanned once per
// enclosing loop. So D nested loops make the scan O(depth x tokens) — quadratic in TIME.
//
// ⚠ IT WILL LIKELY READ ~LINEAR HERE ANYWAY, AND THAT IS THE POINT OF THE BLIND-SPOT DECLARATION. The
// scan allocates ~one small `AssignedNameSet` per loop and mints one phi per carried var per loop —
// both LINEAR in depth — so its cost is in TIME, which scale-test does not measure. The knob exists so
// the shape is present the day the scan starts ALLOCATING per token; until then the manifest names it
// as the canonical example of a superlinearity this instrument cannot see. See `printCorpusManifest`.
//
// Its base is the smallest of any knob: the loops all carry the SINGLE accumulator `acc`, so peak
// register pressure is one value at ANY depth (an E5001-safe deep nest — a per-level counter would be
// live across every enclosing loop and trip E5001 at depth ~14). Base 2 reaches depth 64 at rung 5 —
// realistic in shape (real code nests loops to depth 3), super-realistic in magnitude, exactly as
// `longFunction`'s 3,200 ifs are.
let LoopNestBase = 2 as KnobSize

// How many SCOPE-FILLER locals a long generated function carries — the V DIMENSION, and it DOUBLES.
//
// ⚠ THIS DOUBLES BECAUSE THE CORPUS WAS BLIND, AND IT WAS BLIND IN THE ONE DIMENSION IT NAMED ITSELF
// FOR. The parser builds SSA on the fly and pays a cost O(vars-in-scope) at every branch: each `if`
// SNAPSHOTS the whole mutable-var set, and — until it was fixed — every `ParseExpression` snapshotted
// the variable registry too. `longFunction` and `deepBlocks` carried a FIXED handful of locals, so V
// never grew and no O(V·anything) cost could ever surface. **A fixed V is a V dimension the ladder
// cannot climb.** The comment that used to sit here defended the constant as "realistic, and that is
// the whole point" — and a review DISPROVED exactly that reasoning: real IS the shape, but the
// MAGNITUDE must be scaled past real, or the instrument sees nothing. `longFunction` already does this
// with 3,200 `if`s; this does it with locals.
//
// So V doubles, and the base is picked so the TOP rung reaches ~4,096 locals — into the thousands,
// which is where the deleted `ParseExpression` snapshot's O(V) cost actually bit (~4,000). Real
// functions top out near ~30 locals (measured across maxon-shv2 + stdlib: p99 = 16, max = 44), so
// this is deliberately super-realistic in magnitude and realistic in shape.
//
// WHAT THE LADDER NOW SEES (measured on current `main`, both V and the ifs doubling):
//   • `phase:parse` ALLOCATIONS grow x2.00 — LINEAR. The `ParseExpression` HashSet snapshot that went
//     O(V) per call is GONE (deleted with the ternary short-circuit), so this column is now a
//     REGRESSION GUARD, and its being linear is that fix being CONFIRMED, not the knob being idle.
//   • `phase:parse` BYTES grow ~x3.9 — QUADRATIC. That is the `if`-half snapshot, which copies the
//     whole mutable-var set (O(V)) at every branch: O(V) per if × O(ifs) ifs = O(V·ifs), and with both
//     doubling that is x4. KNOWN and deliberately unfixed (a surplus copy that mints no ValueId);
//     visible for the first time because V finally moves. Flattening it is a SEPARATE rung.
let LocalsPerFunctionBase = 128 as KnobSize

// The BOUNDED set of accumulators the generated branches actually MUTATE — the "assigned" set, held
// apart from the "in-scope" set above so the two dimensions stay distinct.
//
// It is a small constant on purpose: values live across the branches must fit the 14-GPR pool, so the
// knob measures the PARSER (O(vars in scope)) and never accidentally becomes a second `pressuredLoops`
// by spilling. The V scope-fillers each fold into one of these the instant they are declared (see
// `fillerLocalsDecl`), so a filler's live range is length ~1 and the machine only ever holds these
// six — while all V+6 remain IN SCOPE for every branch's snapshot. That gap between "in scope" (V+6)
// and "live" (6) is the whole trick that lets V climb into the thousands without tripping E5001.
let WorkingLocalsPerFunction = 6 as KnobSize

// Declare the bounded working accumulators — the set the generated branches MUTATE — each seeded from
// the parameter `a` so the folder cannot see through them and fold the function away.
function workingLocalsDecl() returns String
	var out = StringBuilder.create()
	for w in 0 upto WorkingLocalsPerFunction 'decl'
		out.append("\tvar w{w} = a + {w + 1}\n")
	end 'decl'
	return out.build()
end 'workingLocalsDecl'

// Declare `localCount` SCOPE-FILLER locals — the V dimension. Each is folded into a working
// accumulator the instant it is declared, which is load-bearing in two independent ways:
//
//   IN SCOPE, NOT LIVE. Once declared, a filler stays in the parser's mutable-var set until the
//     function ends, so every later branch snapshots it — that is the O(V) parser cost this knob
//     exists to grow. But its VALUE is dead after the fold one line down, so the backend holds it for
//     a single instruction and never spills it. Declaring all V at the top and reading them at the END
//     instead (the old `sum`) makes all V LIVE at once and trips E5001 at V > ~13 — which is why that
//     shape could never have carried a doubling V at all.
//   NOT DEAD. The fold USES the filler, so no dead-code pass may drop it and quietly take the V
//     dimension away again.
function fillerLocalsDecl(localCount KnobSize) returns String
	var out = StringBuilder.create()
	for v in 0 upto localCount 'fill'
		out.append("\tvar l{v} = a + {v + 1}\n")
		out.append("\tw{v mod WorkingLocalsPerFunction} = w{v mod WorkingLocalsPerFunction} + l{v}\n")
	end 'fill'
	return out.build()
end 'fillerLocalsDecl'

// The full local prelude a scaling function opens with: the bounded working set the branches mutate,
// then `localCount` scope-fillers. Shared by `longFunction` and `deepBlocks` so the two measure the
// SAME V dimension against different CFG shapes rather than each spelling the prelude out.
function scopedLocalsDecl(localCount KnobSize) returns String
	var out = StringBuilder.create()
	out.append(workingLocalsDecl())
	out.append(fillerLocalsDecl(localCount))
	return out.build()
end 'scopedLocalsDecl'

// The shared epilogue: sum the bounded working accumulators and RETURN the total. Only these six are
// read here, so peak pressure at the end stays at six — the fillers are long dead and cost nothing.
// It emits the `return` too, so `longFunction` and `deepBlocks` share the whole tail, not just the sum.
function workingLocalsSumAndReturn() returns String
	var out = StringBuilder.create()
	out.append("\tvar sum = a\n")
	for w in 0 upto WorkingLocalsPerFunction 'sum'
		out.append("\tsum = sum + w{w}\n")
	end 'sum'
	out.append("\treturn sum\n")
	return out.build()
end 'workingLocalsSumAndReturn'

// A nesting-indent depth: bounded well above `LoopNestBase << MaxRungCount` plus the couple of levels
// the generator adds, so `d + 2` for the deepest loop stays in range.
typealias TabCount = int(0 to 2097152)

// `count` tab characters — the cosmetic indentation for the nested-loop knob. Maxon blocks are
// delimited by `end 'label'`, not by indentation, so this is purely for a human reading a dumped
// corpus; the parser's loop-phi scan counts `while`/`end` TOKENS and never looks at it.
function tabs(count TabCount) returns String
	var out = StringBuilder.create()
	var n = count
	while n > 0 'each'
		out.append("\t")
		n = n - 1
	end 'each'
	return out.build()
end 'tabs'

// How many accumulators each pressured loop holds live ACROSS a call.
//
// EIGHT IS A CHOSEN NUMBER, not a round one. The allocatable pool is 14 GPRs, of which only 5 are
// callee-saved — so a value live across a call has just those 5 to live in, and the excess MUST go
// to memory. Eight accumulators plus the loop counter plus the running total is ten values live
// across the call: five forced spill brackets per loop, which is exactly the splitter work this
// knob exists to provoke.
//
// And it stays clear of E5001. Peak pressure here is eleven (the ten above plus the call's result),
// against a full pool of fourteen — so the loop FITS the machine, and the spills are the ABI's
// doing, not a register shortage. Raising this to fourteen-plus would make the corpus fail to
// compile with a register-pressure diagnostic and VOID the whole ladder. See
// `specs-shv2/register-pressure.md`, which spells out that a forced spill across a call is not
// E5001 and must never become it.
let PressuredValuesPerLoop = 8 as KnobSize

// Iterations of each generated loop. Small: the compiler never RUNS these programs, so trip count
// is irrelevant to the measurement — only the code shape is. Big enough not to look degenerate.
let GeneratedLoopTrips = 4

// ===========================================================================================
// Rung layout on disk
// ===========================================================================================

// Shared with ScalePerType, which locates the rungs this generator wrote, and with the runner, which
// names each rung's metrics + output file after it — in the corpus ROOT, never in the rung.
export let RungDirPrefix = "rung"
export let MaxonExtension = ".maxon"

// One generated rung: where it lives and how big it is. `sourceBytes` is the corpus self-check's
// denominator — the question "did the compiler's output grow with its input" needs both.
export type ScaleRung
	export let index as RungIndex
	export let dir as FilePath
	export let sourceBytes as SourceByteCount

	export static function create(index RungIndex, dir FilePath, sourceBytes SourceByteCount) returns ScaleRung
		return Self{index: index, dir: dir, sourceBytes: sourceBytes}
	end 'create'
end 'ScaleRung'

export typealias ScaleRungArray = Array with ScaleRung

// A knob's size at `rung`: `base << rung`. THE one place the doubling lives, so no knob can drift
// out of step with the others and quietly change what "one rung" means.
function knobAt(base KnobSize, rung RungIndex) returns KnobSize
	var size = base
	for _ in 0 upto rung 'double'
		size = size * 2
	end 'double'
	return size
end 'knobAt'

// Generate the whole ladder under `root`, returning one ScaleRung per rung.
export function generateCorpus(root FilePath, rungs RungCount) returns ScaleRungArray throws ScaleError
	if not Directory.create(root) 'noRoot'
		throw ScaleError.reason("could not create the corpus root {root.toString()}")
	end 'noRoot'

	var ladder = ScaleRungArray.create()
	for rung in 0 upto rungs 'eachRung'
		ladder.push(try generateRung(root, rung: rung as RungIndex))
	end 'eachRung'
	return ladder
end 'generateCorpus'

// Generate one rung: a DIRECTORY of `.maxon` files (shv2's `build <dir>` walks them recursively),
// so the `manyFiles` knob is expressed as real files on disk rather than simulated.
function generateRung(root FilePath, rung RungIndex) returns ScaleRung throws ScaleError
	let dirName = try FilePath.from("{RungDirPrefix}{rung}") otherwise panic("ScaleCorpus: rung dir name must be valid")
	let dir = root.join(dirName)
	if not Directory.create(dir) 'noDir'
		throw ScaleError.reason("could not create the rung directory {dir.toString()}")
	end 'noDir'

	let fileCount = knobAt(FilesBase, rung: rung)
	let funcCount = knobAt(FunctionsBase, rung: rung)
	let localCount = knobAt(LocalsPerFunctionBase, rung: rung)

	var sourceBytes = 0 as SourceByteCount
	var written = StringArray.create()
	// The `manyFunctions` names, in call order — filled by `writeManyFunctions`, read back by `main`.
	var funcNames = StringArray.create()

	// `a_` / `b_` / ... prefixes only order the directory listing for a human reading a dumped
	// corpus; shv2 merges files in listing order and the merge is order-independent.
	sourceBytes = sourceBytes + try writeRungFile(dir, name: "a_seed", source: seedSource(), written: written)
	sourceBytes = sourceBytes + try writeManyFunctions(dir, fileCount: fileCount, funcCount: funcCount, written: written, funcNames: funcNames)
	sourceBytes = sourceBytes + try writeRungFile(dir, name: "c_long", source: longFunctionSource(knobAt(LongIfsBase, rung: rung), localCount: localCount), written: written)
	sourceBytes = sourceBytes + try writeRungFile(dir, name: "d_blocks", source: deepBlocksSource(knobAt(DeepBlocksBase, rung: rung), localCount: localCount), written: written)
	sourceBytes = sourceBytes + try writeRungFile(dir, name: "e_chain", source: callChainSource(knobAt(CallChainBase, rung: rung)), written: written)
	sourceBytes = sourceBytes + try writeRungFile(dir, name: "f_loops", source: pressuredLoopsSource(knobAt(PressuredLoopsBase, rung: rung)), written: written)
	sourceBytes = sourceBytes + try writeRungFile(dir, name: "g_nested", source: nestedLoopsSource(knobAt(LoopNestBase, rung: rung)), written: written)
	sourceBytes = sourceBytes + try writeRungFile(dir, name: "h_shifts", source: shiftCoverageSource(), written: written)
	sourceBytes = sourceBytes + try writeRungFile(dir, name: "i_main", source: mainSource(funcNames, chainLength: knobAt(CallChainBase, rung: rung)), written: written)

	try pruneRungDir(dir, generated: written)

	return ScaleRung.create(rung, dir: dir, sourceBytes: sourceBytes)
end 'generateRung'

// Write one generated file, returning its byte length so the caller can total the rung's source
// size without stat-ing the directory back, and recording its NAME so the prune below knows it
// belongs there.
function writeRungFile(dir FilePath, name String, source String, written StringArray) returns SourceByteCount throws ScaleError
	let fileName = "{name}{MaxonExtension}"
	let path = dir.join(fileName)
	try File.writeText(path, content: source) otherwise throw ScaleError.reason("could not write {path.toString()}")
	written.push(fileName)
	return source.byteLength() as SourceByteCount
end 'writeRungFile'

// Delete everything in the rung directory that this run did not just generate — see the header.
//
// A rung directory is not a place, it is a VALUE: it is exactly the program this rung's knobs
// describe. Anything else in it — a stale `b_funcs_9.maxon` from a run with a bigger `FilesBase`, an
// executable an older version of the runner wrote there — is compiled or listed by `build <dir>`, and
// so is part of the measurement, silently and unreproducibly.
//
// A stale SUBDIRECTORY is a THROW rather than a delete: `build <dir>` walks recursively, so its
// contents would be compiled, and the stdlib has no recursive remove with which to fix it. Refusing
// with the path is the honest answer; the fix is one `rm -rf` and it should be a person's decision,
// not this generator's.
function pruneRungDir(dir FilePath, generated StringArray) throws ScaleError
	let entries = try Directory.list(dir) otherwise throw ScaleError.reason("could not list the rung directory {dir.toString()}")

	for entry in entries 'each'
		if Directory.isDirectory(entry) 'staleSubdir'
			throw ScaleError.reason("{entry.toString()} is a directory inside a rung, and `build <dir>` walks recursively — so it would be COMPILED as part of this rung and the measurement would be of a program this generator did not write. Delete the corpus root and re-run.")
		end 'staleSubdir'

		if generated.contains(entry.filename()) 'generated'
			continue
		end 'generated'

		try File.delete(entry) otherwise throw ScaleError.reason("could not delete the stale file {entry.toString()} from the rung directory, and leaving it there would put it in the measurement")
	end 'each'
end 'pruneRungDir'

// ===========================================================================================
// The knobs, as source
// ===========================================================================================

// THE ORIGIN OF EVERY VALUE IN THE CORPUS, and the reason none of it folds away.
//
// `acc` is a loop-carried `var`: at the top of the loop it is a phi of its initializer and what the
// body last wrote it. `foldConstOperands` moves a CONSTANT operand into an immediate; it cannot
// prove a phi is a constant, and shv2 has no SCCP that could. So `scaleSeed()` is opaque, its
// return value is opaque, and everything downstream that derives from it is opaque too.
//
// This is the same trick `specs-shv2/register-pressure.md` uses to hold 17 values live without the
// folder eating them.
function seedSource() returns String
	var out = StringBuilder.create()
	out.append("// Generated by ScaleCorpus. The opaque seed every other value derives from.\n")
	out.append("function scaleSeed() returns int\n")
	out.append("\tvar acc = 1\n")
	out.append("\tvar i = 0\n")
	out.append("\twhile i < {GeneratedLoopTrips} 'seedLoop'\n")
	out.append("\t\tacc = acc + i * 3\n")
	out.append("\t\ti = i + 1\n")
	out.append("\tend 'seedLoop'\n")
	out.append("\treturn acc\n")
	out.append("end 'scaleSeed'\n")
	out.append("\n")
	// A callee with a branch in it, so a call to it is neither trivially foldable nor free. Every
	// pressured loop calls this ACROSS its live accumulators, which is what forces the spills.
	out.append("function scaleOpaque(a int) returns int\n")
	out.append("\tif a > 100 'big'\n")
	out.append("\t\treturn a - 100\n")
	out.append("\tend 'big'\n")
	out.append("\treturn a + 1\n")
	out.append("end 'scaleOpaque'\n")
	return out.build()
end 'seedSource'

// The `manyFunctions` + `manyFiles` knobs, which are one piece of work: N functions spread over M
// real files. Both double per rung, so functions-per-file is constant and the two knobs stay
// independent — M is what `load`/`merge`/the query spine see, N is what the pass pipeline sees.
//
// It appends every function's NAME to `funcNames`, in the order it generated them. `main` calls them
// back from that list — so the enumeration (the ceiling division and the `>= funcCount` break) lives
// HERE, once. When it lived twice, `main`'s copy had to reproduce this one's names exactly or it would
// call an undefined `sfX_Y` (a broken run) or skip one (an uncalled function a future DCE deletes,
// silently flattening the knob — the very failure `mainSource` guards against).
function writeManyFunctions(dir FilePath, fileCount KnobSize, funcCount KnobSize, written StringArray, funcNames StringArray) returns SourceByteCount throws ScaleError
	// Ceiling division: the last file takes the remainder, and no function is dropped because the
	// counts did not divide evenly.
	let perFile = (funcCount + fileCount - 1) / fileCount

	var total = 0 as SourceByteCount
	for fileIndex in 0 upto fileCount 'eachFile'
		var out = StringBuilder.create()
		out.append("// Generated by ScaleCorpus: the many-small-functions knob, file {fileIndex}.\n")
		for slot in 0 upto perFile 'eachFunc'
			let funcIndex = fileIndex * perFile + slot
			if funcIndex >= funcCount 'past'
				break
			end 'past'
			funcNames.push(smallFunctionName(fileIndex, slot: slot))
			out.append(smallFunctionSource(fileIndex, slot: slot, salt: funcIndex))
		end 'eachFunc'
		total = total + try writeRungFile(dir, name: "b_funcs_{fileIndex}", source: out.build(), written: written)
	end 'eachFile'
	return total
end 'writeManyFunctions'

// One small function of the realistic-codebase shape: a couple of arithmetic ops and a guarded
// branch. Everything derives from the parameter `a`, which is a call argument at every call site and
// therefore opaque to the folder.
//
// The guard is a SHORT-CIRCUIT `and`. This is the knob that doubles the FUNCTION COUNT, so it is
// where a per-function cost shows up — and short-circuit lowering is not free: it mints two blocks, a
// merge phi and a seed literal per `and`. The corpus contained no `and`/`or` at all until 2026-07-14,
// which meant the compare/branch fusion path — the commonest conditional shape in the language — was
// measured by nothing.
function smallFunctionSource(fileIndex KnobSize, slot KnobSize, salt KnobSize) returns String
	let name = smallFunctionName(fileIndex, slot: slot)
	// `salt` varies the constants across functions so they are not textually identical — identical
	// bodies would be a fair benchmark of nothing but the parser's cache.
	let k = salt mod 7 + 1
	var out = StringBuilder.create()
	out.append("\nfunction {name}(a int) returns int\n")
	out.append("\tlet b = a + {k}\n")
	out.append("\tlet c = b * 3\n")
	out.append("\tif c > a and b < c 'grew'\n")
	out.append("\t\treturn c - a\n")
	out.append("\tend 'grew'\n")
	out.append("\treturn c + a\n")
	out.append("end '{name}'\n")
	return out.build()
end 'smallFunctionSource'

function smallFunctionName(fileIndex KnobSize, slot KnobSize) returns String
	return "sf{fileIndex}_{slot}"
end 'smallFunctionName'

// The `longFunction` knob: ONE function, N sequential `if`s, over a bounded working set and
// `localCount` scope-fillers. At rung 5 this is the 3,200-`if` benchmark ARCHITECTURE.md quotes a
// measurement for. It grows OPS in a near-straight line; the CFG grows with it but stays a simple
// chain.
//
// Three things here are load-bearing, and the knob measured nothing without the first two (see
// `LocalsPerFunctionBase`):
//
//   • `localCount` SCOPE-FILLERS, so V climbs and the parser's per-branch O(vars-in-scope) snapshot
//     has something to climb WITH. Every filler is in scope at every `if` below it, and there are V of
//     them at the top rung. It used to be a fixed handful.
//   • Each branch mutates a ROTATING SUBSET of the bounded WORKING set, never the whole thing. A branch
//     that wrote every var in scope would make "cost O(vars in scope)" and "cost O(vars assigned)" the
//     same number, and the corpus could not tell the two apart — precisely the distinction that
//     decides whether a write-trail is worth building. Here in-scope is V+6 and assigned is ≤3.
//   • The guard is a SHORT-CIRCUIT `and`, which is what real code writes and which — measured
//     2026-07-14, while fixing the `const` flags bug — appeared in this corpus exactly nowhere. A
//     codegen change to the commonest conditional shape in the language was invisible to the instrument.
function longFunctionSource(ifCount KnobSize, localCount KnobSize) returns String
	var out = StringBuilder.create()
	out.append("// Generated by ScaleCorpus: the long-function knob — {ifCount} sequential ifs over {WorkingLocalsPerFunction} working locals and {localCount} scope-fillers.\n")
	out.append("function scaleLongIf(a int) returns int\n")
	out.append(scopedLocalsDecl(localCount))
	for i in 0 upto ifCount 'eachIf'
		let p = i mod WorkingLocalsPerFunction
		let q = (i + 1) mod WorkingLocalsPerFunction
		let r = (i + 2) mod WorkingLocalsPerFunction
		out.append("\tif w{p} > {i} and w{q} < w{r} 'c{i}'\n")
		out.append("\t\tw{p} = w{p} + {i mod 5 + 1}\n")
		out.append("\t\tw{q} = w{q} + w{r}\n")
		out.append("\tend 'c{i}'\n")
	end 'eachIf'
	out.append(workingLocalsSumAndReturn())
	out.append("end 'scaleLongIf'\n")
	return out.build()
end 'longFunctionSource'

// The `deepBlocks` knob: ONE function whose BLOCK count is huge. Each if/else is three blocks (then,
// else, continuation), so this lands near 3x its knob — ~19,200 blocks at rung 5, which is the
// shape that used to SIGSEGV and the reason "no CFG traversal may recurse" is a rule rather than a
// preference. Distinct from `longFunction`: that grows ops along a chain, this grows the CFG. It
// carries the same `localCount` scope-fillers, so the V dimension is measured against a DIAMOND CFG
// here and a straight chain there.
// ⚠ THE TWO ARMS MUTATE DIFFERENT WORKING LOCALS, DELIBERATELY. That is what makes the merge do real
// work: each arm leaves a different subset changed, so the join genuinely has to reconcile them. When
// both arms wrote the SAME single `acc`, every merge was the trivial one-phi case and the
// snapshot-and-reconcile cost this knob is supposed to provoke never appeared.
function deepBlocksSource(ifElseCount KnobSize, localCount KnobSize) returns String
	var out = StringBuilder.create()
	out.append("// Generated by ScaleCorpus: the deep-blocks knob — {ifElseCount} if/elses, ~{ifElseCount * 3} blocks, {WorkingLocalsPerFunction} working locals and {localCount} scope-fillers.\n")
	out.append("function scaleDeepBlocks(a int) returns int\n")
	out.append(scopedLocalsDecl(localCount))
	for i in 0 upto ifElseCount 'eachIfElse'
		let p = i mod WorkingLocalsPerFunction
		let q = (i + 1) mod WorkingLocalsPerFunction
		out.append("\tif w{p} > {i} 'd{i}'\n")
		out.append("\t\tw{p} = w{p} + {i mod 3 + 1}\n")
		out.append("\tend 'd{i}' else 'e{i}'\n")
		out.append("\t\tw{q} = w{q} - {i mod 2 + 1}\n")
		out.append("\tend 'e{i}'\n")
	end 'eachIfElse'
	out.append(workingLocalsSumAndReturn())
	out.append("end 'scaleDeepBlocks'\n")
	return out.build()
end 'deepBlocksSource'

// The `callChain` knob: N functions, each calling the one below it and holding values LIVE ACROSS
// that call. The live values are the point — a bare call chain would measure call lowering, but
// values live across a call can only live in the 5 callee-saved registers, so this is what
// exercises the cross-call spill bracket.
function callChainSource(length KnobSize) returns String
	var out = StringBuilder.create()
	out.append("// Generated by ScaleCorpus: the call-chain knob — {length} chained calls, values live across each.\n")
	// The base of the chain: takes no call, so the recursion of generated text terminates.
	out.append("function scaleChain0(a int) returns int\n")
	out.append("\treturn a + 1\n")
	out.append("end 'scaleChain0'\n")

	for i in 1 upto length 'eachLink'
		out.append("\nfunction scaleChain{i}(a int) returns int\n")
		out.append("\tlet x = a + {i mod 9 + 1}\n")
		out.append("\tlet y = a * 2\n")
		out.append("\tlet z = scaleChain{i - 1}(a)\n")
		out.append("\treturn x + y + z\n")
		out.append("end 'scaleChain{i}'\n")
	end 'eachLink'
	return out.build()
end 'callChainSource'

// The `pressuredLoops` knob: ONE function, N loops, each holding PressuredValuesPerLoop
// accumulators live across a call to `scaleOpaque`. The splitter must spill the accumulators that do
// not fit the five callee-saved registers, so this is the knob that exercises the splitter hardest.
//
// ⚠ IT IS LINEAR NOW — see `PressuredLoopsBase`, which carries the full history. It USED to be the
// superlinear knob, because the splitter's driver recomputed liveness from scratch after every split
// (O(function × splits)); `49b160ce2` made that driver incremental and `regalloc:splitting` /
// `regalloc:liveness` both read ~x2.0 since. Do not restore a "KNOWN SUPERLINEAR" claim here without
// measuring it: that claim outlived its bug twice already (once here, once at the parser).
//
// It does NOT trip E5001 — see PressuredValuesPerLoop.
function pressuredLoopsSource(loopCount KnobSize) returns String
	var out = StringBuilder.create()
	out.append("// Generated by ScaleCorpus: the pressured-loops knob — {loopCount} loops, {PressuredValuesPerLoop} values live across a call in each.\n")
	out.append("function scalePressured(a int) returns int\n")
	out.append("\tvar total = a\n")

	for loopIndex in 0 upto loopCount 'eachLoop'
		// Fresh accumulator names per loop, so the loops' live ranges do not overlap and peak
		// pressure stays at one loop's worth however many loops there are.
		for v in 0 upto PressuredValuesPerLoop 'declValues'
			out.append("\tvar s{loopIndex}_{v} = a + {v + 1}\n")
		end 'declValues'
		out.append("\tvar i{loopIndex} = 0\n")
		out.append("\twhile i{loopIndex} < {GeneratedLoopTrips} 'p{loopIndex}'\n")
		// The call is what makes the loop pressured: every accumulator is live across it, and a
		// value live across a call is forbidden all 9 caller-saved registers.
		out.append("\t\tlet r{loopIndex} = scaleOpaque(i{loopIndex})\n")
		for v in 0 upto PressuredValuesPerLoop 'updateValues'
			out.append("\t\ts{loopIndex}_{v} = s{loopIndex}_{v} + r{loopIndex}\n")
		end 'updateValues'
		out.append("\t\ti{loopIndex} = i{loopIndex} + 1\n")
		out.append("\tend 'p{loopIndex}'\n")

		for v in 0 upto PressuredValuesPerLoop 'sumValues'
			out.append("\ttotal = total + s{loopIndex}_{v}\n")
		end 'sumValues'
	end 'eachLoop'

	out.append("\treturn total\n")
	out.append("end 'scalePressured'\n")
	return out.build()
end 'pressuredLoopsSource'

// The condition every nested loop tests. Large enough that the loop is not trivially one iteration —
// the compiler never RUNS these programs, so the exact bound is irrelevant to the measurement; only
// the loop's SHAPE is. It is compared against the opaque `acc`, so the folder cannot see through it.
let NestedLoopLimit = 1000000

// The `loopNest` knob: ONE function, ONE chain of loops NESTED to `depth`. Distinct from
// `pressuredLoops` (loop COUNT at depth 1): this grows loop DEPTH, which is what the loop-phi token
// pre-scan climbs — `parseWhileStatement` re-scans a loop's whole token extent, and a loop nested
// inside another is re-scanned once per enclosing loop, so this drives that scan O(depth x tokens).
//
// EVERY loop tests and mutates the SINGLE carried accumulator `acc`. That is deliberate: it keeps peak
// register pressure at one value however deep the nest goes (a per-level counter would be live across
// every enclosing loop and trip E5001 at depth ~14 — see `LoopNestBase`), and it keeps the loops
// fold-resistant, because `acc` originates in the opaque seed.
//
// ⚠ EXPECT THIS TO READ ~LINEAR IN MEMORY EVEN IF THE SCAN IS QUADRATIC IN TIME. The scan allocates
// ~one `AssignedNameSet` per loop (linear in depth) and each loop mints one phi for `acc` (linear in
// depth); its O(depth x tokens) cost is spent WALKING tokens, which allocates nothing. scale-test
// measures memory only, so a time-only superlinearity is invisible here BY DESIGN — this knob is the
// manifest's worked example of that blind spot, not a curve expected to bend.
function nestedLoopsSource(depth KnobSize) returns String
	var out = StringBuilder.create()
	out.append("// Generated by ScaleCorpus: the loop-nest knob — one chain of {depth} loops nested to depth {depth}, all carrying one accumulator.\n")
	out.append("function scaleNested(a int) returns int\n")
	out.append("\tvar acc = a\n")

	// One pass: append each loop's opener, and PREPEND its `end` to a separate string. Prepending is
	// what puts the closers in innermost-first order (`n{depth-1}` … `n0`) with no reverse index — a
	// backward count would have to reach 0, which a ranged loop counter cannot represent.
	var closes = ""
	for d in 0 upto depth 'open'
		out.append("{tabs(d + 1)}while acc < {NestedLoopLimit} 'n{d}'\n")
		out.append("{tabs(d + 2)}acc = acc + {d + 1}\n")
		closes = "{tabs(d + 1)}end 'n{d}'\n{closes}"
	end 'open'

	out.append(closes)
	out.append("\treturn acc\n")
	out.append("end 'scaleNested'\n")
	return out.build()
end 'nestedLoopsSource'

// The mask that bounds a dynamic shift COUNT to a sane, non-negative range. A count derived this way
// is a runtime value the folder cannot see, so it takes the GUARDED shift path — the 6-7 ops that
// saturate an out-of-range count — which a constant count folds away and which nothing in this corpus
// exercised before. This is also the corpus's bitwise `and` on plain ints (the guards' `and` is the
// short-circuit one, over bools).
let ShiftCountMask = 63

// The shift COVERAGE shapes. These do NOT scale — they are about COVERAGE, not the ladder: their
// value is that a future codegen change to shifts is no longer invisible, the way the `const`-flags
// fix was invisible because the corpus had no `and`/`or`. A shift codegen change moves these functions'
// emitted bytes, and the trend delta reports ANY movement, constant or not.
//
// Four axes are covered that the corpus had at ZERO before:
//   • CONSTANT count (folded at compile time) vs DYNAMIC count (a runtime value, guarded).
//   • SIGNED operand (arithmetic / sign fill) vs RANGED `int(0 to u64.max)` operand (logical / zero
//     fill). The fill is decided by the LEFT operand's type, and this is the shape that once hid a
//     fold-vs-codegen disagreement (const=0 vs dyn=-4294967296).
function shiftCoverageSource() returns String
	var out = StringBuilder.create()
	out.append("// Generated by ScaleCorpus: shift COVERAGE (fixed, not scaled) — const/dynamic counts over signed and ranged operands.\n")
	out.append("typealias ScaleWide = int(0 to u64.max)\n")
	out.append("typealias ScaleSigned = int(i64.min to i64.max)\n")
	out.append("\n")

	// Dynamic count, signed operand: the guarded shl then shr.
	out.append("function scaleShiftDyn(v ScaleSigned, c ScaleSigned) returns int\n")
	out.append("\tlet x = v shl c\n")
	out.append("\treturn x shr c\n")
	out.append("end 'scaleShiftDyn'\n")
	out.append("\n")

	// Constant count, ranged (unsigned) operand: zero-fill decided by the LEFT operand's range.
	out.append("function scaleShiftWideConst(v ScaleWide) returns int\n")
	out.append("\treturn v shr 60\n")
	out.append("end 'scaleShiftWideConst'\n")
	out.append("\n")

	// Dynamic count, ranged operand: guarded, and the fill still decided by the operand's range.
	out.append("function scaleShiftWideDyn(v ScaleWide, c ScaleSigned) returns int\n")
	out.append("\treturn v shr c\n")
	out.append("end 'scaleShiftWideDyn'\n")
	out.append("\n")

	// Constant count, signed operand: arithmetic (sign) fill.
	out.append("function scaleShiftSignedConst(v ScaleSigned) returns int\n")
	out.append("\treturn v shr 60\n")
	out.append("end 'scaleShiftSignedConst'\n")
	out.append("\n")

	// The driver threads the opaque seed through every shift function. The seed `a` flows straight into
	// every operand, signed OR ranged: shv2's argument check is CLASS-based (numeric agrees with
	// numeric), so a plain `int` coerces into a `ScaleWide` parameter with no cast and no mask. The
	// operand's VALUE never matters here — the shift codegen inside each callee is exercised because the
	// parameter is opaque (shv2 has no interprocedural constant propagation), not because of what flows
	// in. Dynamic counts are the seed masked to a small non-negative range so they stay runtime values.
	out.append("function scaleShifts(a int) returns int\n")
	out.append("\tvar acc = a\n")
	out.append("\tacc = acc + scaleShiftWideConst(a)\n")
	out.append("\tacc = acc + scaleShiftWideDyn(a, c: acc and {ShiftCountMask})\n")
	out.append("\tacc = acc + scaleShiftSignedConst(acc)\n")
	out.append("\tacc = acc + scaleShiftDyn(acc, c: acc and {ShiftCountMask})\n")
	out.append("\tacc = acc + (acc shl 3)\n")
	out.append("\tacc = acc + (acc shr 2)\n")
	out.append("\treturn acc\n")
	out.append("end 'scaleShifts'\n")
	return out.build()
end 'shiftCoverageSource'

// `main`, which threads the seed through every generated function.
//
// It calls all of them ON PURPOSE. An uncalled function is a function a future dead-code pass could
// legitimately delete, and the day that pass lands, the `manyFunctions` knob would silently
// evaporate and the ladder would go flat — reporting perfect linearity because it had stopped
// compiling anything. Threading `acc` through every call makes each one load-bearing.
//
// The `manyFunctions` names come from `funcNames`, the exact list `writeManyFunctions` generated — so
// the enumeration is not written down a second time here and cannot drift from the names on disk.
function mainSource(funcNames StringArray, chainLength KnobSize) returns String
	var out = StringBuilder.create()
	out.append("// Generated by ScaleCorpus: threads the opaque seed through every generated function.\n")
	out.append("function main() returns ExitCode\n")
	out.append("\tvar acc = scaleSeed()\n")

	for name in funcNames 'eachFunc'
		out.append("\tacc = {name}(acc)\n")
	end 'eachFunc'

	out.append("\tacc = scaleLongIf(acc)\n")
	out.append("\tacc = scaleDeepBlocks(acc)\n")
	out.append("\tacc = scaleChain{chainLength - 1}(acc)\n")
	out.append("\tacc = scalePressured(acc)\n")
	out.append("\tacc = scaleNested(acc)\n")
	out.append("\tacc = scaleShifts(acc)\n")
	out.append("\tif acc > 0 'ok'\n")
	out.append("\t\treturn 0\n")
	out.append("\tend 'ok'\n")
	out.append("\treturn 1\n")
	out.append("end 'main'\n")
	return out.build()
end 'mainSource'

// ===========================================================================================
// The corpus self-check — THE reason anything the instrument reports can be believed
// ===========================================================================================
//
// The trap in this file's header is not hypothetical, and this is where it is caught. A corpus that
// folds away leaves the instrument measuring an empty compile at every rung; it would fit a perfect
// straight line through six near-zeroes and report flawless linearity. That is the most convincing
// possible wrong answer, and nothing downstream could catch it — every number would look lovely.
//
// So before anything is reported: did the compiler actually EMIT anything, and did it emit
// proportionally more of it as the program grew? Emitted code bytes are fitted against source bytes
// exactly as a phase's cost is, and a flat or sublinear result means the RUN IS BROKEN.
//
// This is the one condition the instrument exits NON-ZERO for, and the distinction is the whole
// design: a surprising number is a reading to explain, but a degenerate corpus did not produce a
// surprising reading — it produced no reading at all, about a compiler it never ran on a program
// that was never there.

// The floor each rung's emitted code must clear against the rung below it.
//
// THE LADDER DOUBLES, so a corpus that is really being compiled emits about TWICE the code at each
// rung — measured, x1.99 to x2.00 at every step. A corpus that folded away emits about the SAME code
// at every rung (x1.00), because what it emits is the fixed floor and nothing else. x1.50 sits in the
// wide gap between those two and is nowhere near either.
//
// It is deliberately not x2.00: real code size grows slightly SUBLINEARLY, because a rung's fixed
// overhead (the entry stub, the runtime floor) is amortized over more program as the ladder climbs.
export let MinCodeGrowthRatio = 150 as GrowthRatio

// A growth check needs two rungs. `--rungs=1` is a legal ladder — it measures one program, and its
// per-rung numbers are as exact as any other run's — but nothing about GROWTH can be said from it,
// this check included. The runner says so; it does not fail the run for a ladder it was asked for.
export let MinRungsForGrowth = 2

// EVERY rung is checked against the one below it, not just the ends of the ladder.
//
// That is strictly stronger than what it replaced (a power law fitted through all six code sizes,
// whose exponent had to clear a floor): a single rung that folded while its neighbours grew is a rung
// that stopped measuring the compiler, and a fit through the other five would absorb it and report a
// healthy exponent. A ratio per rung cannot absorb anything.
export function assertCorpusCompiled(results RungResultArray) throws ScaleError
	if results.count() < MinRungsForGrowth 'tooFewRungs'
		return
	end 'tooFewRungs'

	for i in 1 upto results.count() 'eachRung'
		let below = try results.get(i - 1) otherwise panic("assertCorpusCompiled: i >= 1 but results.get({i - 1}) failed")
		let rung = try results.get(i) otherwise panic("assertCorpusCompiled: i < count but results.get({i}) failed")

		if not grewBy(rung.codeBytes, previous: below.codeBytes, minimum: MinCodeGrowthRatio) 'folded'
			throw ScaleError.reason("rung {rung.rung} emitted {rung.codeBytes} bytes of code against rung {below.rung}'s {below.codeBytes} — a growth of {growthText(growthOf(rung.codeBytes, previous: below.codeBytes))} where the ladder DOUBLES the program and the floor is {growthText(GrowthReading.measured(MinCodeGrowthRatio))}. The corpus FOLDED AWAY rather than compiling, so every number this run produced is a number about nothing. The generated programs must be fold-resistant (see this file's header): seed every value from a loop-carried accumulator or a call result.")
		end 'folded'
	end 'eachRung'
end 'assertCorpusCompiled'

// The corpus's own growth, rung over rung — PRINTED, and in `--result-json`, because a reader looking
// at a set of memory numbers is entitled to see the evidence that they are numbers about a program
// that was actually compiled rather than one that folded away.
export function codeGrowthAt(results RungResultArray, rung LadderIndex) returns GrowthReading
	if rung == 0 'bottomRung'
		return GrowthReading.noBaseline
	end 'bottomRung'

	let below = try results.get(rung - 1) otherwise panic("codeGrowthAt: rung >= 1 but results.get({rung - 1}) failed")
	let here = try results.get(rung) otherwise panic("codeGrowthAt: results.get({rung}) failed")
	return growthOf(here.codeBytes, previous: below.codeBytes)
end 'codeGrowthAt'

// ===========================================================================================
// THE INSTRUMENT DECLARES WHAT IT CAN SEE — AND WHAT IT STRUCTURALLY CANNOT
// ===========================================================================================
//
// PRINTED ON EVERY RUN. An instrument that cannot state its own blind spots produces confident zeros:
// this corpus was blind in four measured dimensions at once — it grew no locals, nested no loops,
// shifted by no runtime value, and shifted no ranged operand — and every one of those blind spots was
// SILENT. Two independent reviews found five compiler defects the corpus could see NONE of. The
// through-line was always the same: the corpus finds only bugs someone already thought to generate. So
// the corpus now SAYS what it generates and, more importantly, what it does not and what it cannot —
// because the gaps are the part a reader cannot otherwise know, and a gap nobody is told about reads as
// coverage.
//
// TWO of these are STRUCTURAL — no knob can close them, so they are stated as blind spots rather than
// filed as TODOs:
//   • MEMORY ONLY. scale-test measures allocations and bytes, never time; a time-only superlinearity
//     is invisible here by construction, and the loop-nest knob's own scan is the worked example.
//   • THE SUBSET. shv2 compiles a fragment of Maxon, so whole language features generate nothing at
//     all, and a codegen change to any of them moves no number here.
export function printCorpusManifest()
	print("\n")
	print("scale-test: CORPUS MANIFEST — what this ladder exercises, and what it structurally cannot see.\n")
	print("\n")
	print("  CONSTRUCTS GENERATED (a codegen change to one of these moves the memory numbers):\n")
	print("    int arithmetic (+ - *), comparisons, let / var, return\n")
	print("    if, if/else, while, and NESTED while (to a doubling depth)\n")
	print("    functions (<=6 params), cross-file merge, chained calls holding values live across them\n")
	print("    short-circuit `and` (the two-condition guards), bitwise `and` (shift-count masks)\n")
	print("    shl / shr with: a CONSTANT count (folded) AND a DYNAMIC count (runtime-guarded), over a\n")
	print("      SIGNED operand (arithmetic/sign fill) AND a ranged int(0 to u64.max) operand (zero fill)\n")
	print("    ranged typealiases\n")
	print("\n")
	print("  COST DIMENSIONS ON THE DOUBLING LADDER (each DOUBLES per rung; the rung-over-rung RATIO is\n")
	print("  the growth — x2 linear, x4 quadratic — read straight off the counts):\n")
	print("    function count, file count, sequential-if ops, block/CFG count, call-chain depth,\n")
	print("    pressured-loop COUNT, loop-nest DEPTH, and locals-in-scope per function (the V dimension)\n")
	print("\n")
	print("  NOT GENERATED — a codegen change to any of these is INVISIBLE here (structural blind spot):\n")
	print("    `break` (in the subset, but no generated loop exits early); integer `/` and `mod` (idiv);\n")
	print("    `or` / `xor` / `not`; float arithmetic; print, structs, arrays, strings, `for`, `match`;\n")
	print("    closures, async, generics; `as` casts. What the corpus GENERATES is int `+ - *`, functions,\n")
	print("    if/if-else/while, return/let/var, `and` + `shl`/`shr`, and ranged typealiases — nothing else.\n")
	print("\n")
	print("  !! MEASURES MEMORY ONLY — a TIME-only superlinearity is invisible here BY DESIGN. Time is not\n")
	print("     reproducible, so it cannot be trended; a cost that re-walks tokens or chases pointers but\n")
	print("     allocates little will read FLAT in these columns even when it is quadratic in time. The\n")
	print("     worked example is IN this corpus: the loop-nest knob drives the loop-phi token pre-scan\n")
	print("     (Parser.loopBodyEndIndex + namesAssignedIn) to O(depth x tokens), yet that scan allocates\n")
	print("     ~one small set per loop and reads roughly LINEAR here. For the time view of one compile,\n")
	print("     use `--log=compiler:debug` or `--metrics=<path>` — this instrument will not show it.\n")
	print("\n")
end 'printCorpusManifest'
